<header><p class="enc-kicker">Work / monument · text</p><h1 class="enc-title">Carthago delenda est</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Carthago delenda est, &quot;<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/carthage">Carthage</a> must be destroyed&quot;, is a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/latin">Latin</a> phrase associated with the Roman senator <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cato-the-elder">Cato the Elder</a>, who is said to have ended his speeches with it. It expresses Roman hostility to Carthage in the years before the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/third-punic-war">Third Punic War</a>. The war ended with the destruction of Carthage and the end of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/punic-empire">Punic Empire</a> in 146 BCE.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/carthago-delenda-est.jpg" alt="Carthago delenda est" width="271" height="420" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Sailko · CC BY 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Ritratto_maschile_detto_il_vecchio_di_otricoli,_50_ac_ca._(busto_moderno),_da_otricoli_(visconti),_MT533,_05.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
